<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Argentine Civil Wars</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1814">1814</a>–<a href="/years/1880">1880</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Argentine Civil Wars were the series of conflicts fought from 1814 to 1880 between the Unitarians, who wanted a centralised state led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a>, and the Federalists, who defended provincial autonomy. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-manuel-de-rosas">Juan Manuel de Rosas</a> dominated much of the period; his defeat at Caseros in 1852 led to the Constitution of 1853. Buenos Aires joined the republic after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-pavon">Battle of Pavón</a> (1861), and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/federalism">federalisation</a> of the city in 1880 closed the conflicts.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/argentine-civil-wars.jpg" alt="Argentine Civil Wars" width="320" height="417" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Austrian writer Alejandro Bernheim and the Italian cartoonist Carlos Penutti. … · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Guerra_Civil.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The May Revolution of 1810 created the United Provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a>, but the provinces' separate wars of independence did not bring national unity, and the old intendencies broke up into local cabildos that resisted a central government able to appoint and remove their governors. The first open break came in June 1814, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-gervasio-artigas">José Artigas</a> formed the rival Federal League. After the directorship proclaimed the centralising Constitution of 1819, the League's victory at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cepeda-1820">Battle of Cepeda</a> in 1820 dissolved the national government and left personalist strongmen, the caudillos, as the highest authorities in the provinces.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>A federal congress met in 1824 for fear of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, and war with Brazil followed in 1825. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernardino-rivadavia">Bernardino Rivadavia</a>, the first president, passed the Constitution of 1826, which critics called centralist, but resigned after failed peace talks. In December 1828 Juan Lavalle seized power and executed Manuel Dorrego, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-manuel-de-rosas">Juan Manuel de Rosas</a> rose and defeated him. The Unitarian Liga del Interior of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-maria-paz">José María Paz</a>, founded on 5 July 1830, was answered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/federal-pact">Federal Pact</a> of 4 January 1831 and beaten at La Ciudadela. Rosas, granted the sum of public power, ruled the Argentine Confederation until a Brazilian-led alliance defeated him at Caseros in 1852.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a> then refused the San Nicolás Agreement and, after the revolution of 11 September 1852, seceded as a state of its own, keeping control of the customs house, the country's chief revenue. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justo-jose-de-urquiza">Justo José de Urquiza</a> defeated its army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bartolome-mitre">Bartolomé Mitre</a> at Cepeda in 1859 and negotiated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pact-of-san-jose-de-flores">Pact of San José de Flores</a>, but Mitre broke it and won at Pavón in 1861, replacing Federalist governors in more than half of the interior provinces.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Mitre became the republic's first president in 1862, but resistance continued: the Federalist Chacho Peñaloza was killed in 1863, Ricardo López Jordán's rebellion of 1870 to 1876, begun with the assassination of Urquiza, was the last Federalist revolt, and in 1874 Mitre himself rose against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicolas-avellaneda">Nicolás Avellaneda</a>'s inauguration. In 1880 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a> governor Carlos Tejedor took up arms against the election of General Julio Roca. His quick defeat led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/federalism">federalisation</a> of Buenos Aires and to the dominance of Roca's National Autonomist Party until 1916. Throughout, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazilian-empire">Brazilian Empire</a> intervened heavily, and the British and French empires put pressure on the new nations.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Argentine Civil Wars” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The quarrel between Buenos Aires's unitary governments and Artigas's federalism began the <b>Argentine Civil Wars</b> and set the terms of Argentine politics for decades.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-provinces-of-the-rio-de-la-plata">United Provinces of the Río de la Plata · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The coup reignited the <b>Argentine Civil Wars</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-1">1 December in history</a></li></ul></section>
